        <div id="buying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How to Buy a Landice L7</h2>
<p>
            Landice sells direct through its online store and through a network of specialty fitness dealers;
            its store has a dealer finder and a sales line, (973) 927-9010, and accepts HSA and FSA payment.
            We could not find the L7 sold new on Amazon, so the Amazon buttons on this page go to the
            closest alternatives Amazon does sell, not to a Landice.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Price the configuration, not the base.</strong> The console and belt move the price by up to $2,125 on an L7.</li>
            <li><strong>Ask a dealer and Landice for the same configuration.</strong> Dealers may include delivery, installation, a mat or a promotion, and installation can affect labour cover.</li>
            <li><strong>Plan the delivery.</strong> At 345 lb <a href="/how-much-does-a-treadmill-weigh/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>shipping weight</a> and 76 inches long, the L7 needs a clear route and two or more people; it does not fold. Our <a href="/how-to-move-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to moving a treadmill</a> covers stairs and doorways.</li>
            <li><strong>Check power.</strong> Landice specifies a 110 VAC, 15 A supply; a dedicated circuit is safest.</li>
            <li><strong>Consider used or remanufactured.</strong> Landice machines last, so a remanufactured L7 or L8 from a reputable dealer can save thousands. One retailer listed a remanufactured L8 at $3,499 in search results we saw; check what is replaced and what warranty comes with it.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            If you are buying for a clinic, studio or club, the L7 Club ($5,429 to $7,479 on 5 November 2026)
            and L10 are the commercial versions, with commercial warranty terms. Our <a href="/commercial-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">commercial treadmill guide</a> covers that market.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Alternatives to the Landice L7</h2>
<p>
            Few home buyers need what the L7 costs. The questions are whether you need its 400 lb rating and
            build, and whether you can get them for less.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Other light-commercial, non-folding machines.</strong> <a href="/woodway-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Woodway</a>, <a href="/precor-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Precor</a> and <a href="/life-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Life Fitness</a> sell at similar or higher prices, with different warranty structures.</li>
            <li><strong>Heavy-duty home treadmills.</strong> Our <a href="/best-treadmills-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills for heavy people</a> guide ranks machines rated to 400 lb at a fraction of the price, mostly with shorter warranties.</li>
            <li><strong>Running treadmills under $2,000.</strong> For a single runner of average build, a well-built folding machine such as the <a href="/sole-f80-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole F80</a> covers the same speeds and incline with a 60-inch deck.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Landice’s own folding M2 Genesis ($3,999 in our October check) is the cheaper way into the brand,
            covered in our <a href="/landice-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Landice guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Should Buy the Landice L7</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy the L7 if durability is the priority.</strong> A 400 lb rating, an aluminium frame and
            lifetime parts cover for home use are the reasons to pay from $5,429.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy the L8 instead</strong> if you are tall, run with a long stride, weigh close to 400 lb or
            share the machine with someone much larger.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy the L7 RTM</strong> if you or a patient need <a href="/best-treadmills-with-handrails/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>full-length handrails</a>, a very slow start or
            a reversing belt.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Look elsewhere</strong> if you need a folding machine, a 60-inch deck at L7 money, or the
            most features per dollar; and in every case price the exact configuration from Landice and a dealer
            before you decide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying a Landice L7</h2>
          <p>The L7 is expensive enough that small errors cost real money. These are the ones to avoid.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Comparing base prices instead of configurations</h3>
              <p>The L7 runs from $5,429 to $7,804 depending on console, belt and mount. Compare the same configuration across Landice and dealers, and against rivals.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Buying the L7 when you need the L8 deck</h3>
              <p>A 58-inch deck is short for tall runners. If you are over about six feet and run, the L8’s 63-inch deck is the reason to pay $900 more.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Assuming every Landice warranty is lifetime</h3>
              <p>The residential L7 lists lifetime parts, but the RTM lists five years and a dealer listing shows five years. Get the terms for your exact version and installation in writing.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Not measuring the delivery route</h3>
              <p>The L7 does not fold, is 76 inches long and ships at 345 lb. Measure doors, stairs and turns before ordering, and confirm who installs it.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">The Landice L7 is a treadmill for buyers who want one machine for decades</strong>:
            a 400 lb rating, an aluminium frame and lifetime parts cover for home use, from $5,429 to $7,804 on
            5 November 2026. Its 58-inch deck is short for tall runners and it does not fold.
          </p>
          <p>
            Choose the L8 for a 63-inch deck and 500 lb rating, the L7 RTM for clinical handrails and a very slow
            start, and price your exact configuration from Landice and a dealer. Our <a href="/landice-treadmills/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">Landice guide</a> covers the rest of the range.
          </p>
        </div>

